4. The difference between a variable's type and its declaration.

    Consider this statement:

            int     age;

            age  = 20;

                int     the data type,
                age     the variable's name,
                20      the initial value,
                =       the initialization in this declaration

    You can declare multiple variables of the same type:

        int     a;
        int     b;
        int     c;

        a = 1;
        b = 2;
        c = 3;

    And you can declare an varible without initialising it:

        int     x;

    Uninitialised local variables does not automaticly contain ZERO.
    Readng its indeterminate value can cause undefined behavior.



